WEBVTT

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.200
So, I have the subject and objective set for my presentation

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.160 
Now here is my 10-page script

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.220
What? Ten pages? Without any bullet points? 

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.200
What is the structure of your presentation? 

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.130
Yes, before you write the full script,

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.160
it’s better to outline the structure of your presentation.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
What’s the structure of a presentation?

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.090
Basically, it’s the arrangement of the parts 

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.210
in your presentation

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.050 
Divide the presentation into several parts 

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.110
and state the main points in each part

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:47.250
Oh, so it will be more organized and easier to understand?

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:53.050
Usually the structure of a presentation consists of three parts 

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:57.220
an introduction, a body and a closing

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.270
You already know how to make the introduction

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:07.100
Yes. It includes welcoming and introducing your name, title and job

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.190
You also know how to introduce the subject 

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.270
and objectives of your presentation

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.280
And now we’re moving to the second part of the presentation’s structure 

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.100
the body. 

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.200
First, begin with an overview

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.260 
It provides general information about the presentation 

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.200
but not the details

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.000
In other words 

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.230
an overview is a short description of the whole presentation

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.200
Right. After the overview

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.050
establish the content of the presentation

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:47.100
You should decide on the main points and address them separately

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.150
So, if I have three main points, I could say

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000
“First, I’ll be looking at…, second…, and third…“

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.200
You can also number the points under the whole structure

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.110 
Today I will divide my presentation into three main points

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.000 
Point one deals with…  point two…  and point three... 

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.260 
Or you could say, I will begin with….

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.150  
Then I’ll move to.... and I’ll end with....

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.180 
Using this structure

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.130 
there could be sub points that support the main point

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.000  
so the overall structure is well-developed and easy to follow

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.010 
After the body comes the closing

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.000 
It should give the presentation a summary or a conclusion

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.170  
Outlining the structure helps lay a solid foundation

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.170 
before you write the full script

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.220 
It’s kind of like drawing a blueprint for a house, right?

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.280  
First, you decide on how many floors are in the house

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.140  
and then how many rooms are on one floor

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.000 
Last, you plan and work on //the details inside of the structure

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.240 
Right. Now what are you going to do with your 10-page script?

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.120
Good question! I can still use it later

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.070 
But, first things first

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:16.090
I will work on outlining the structure right now

